ion: this fix covers the documented rule (subagent-contract) plus the three originally-reported instances (backlog-item-groomer.md, swarm.md, close/start.md); the four newly-found sites benefit from the same rule once it exists but are not required to be edited for this item to be complete \u2014 recommend a follow-up item to audit and fix them individually.\\nAVAILABLE count: 6\\nDERIVABLE count: 3\\nMISSING count: 0\\nChanges from snapshot:\\n- Condition 6: DERIVABLE \u2192 AVAILABLE (resolved by orchestrator reading subagent-contract/SKILL.md directly)\\n- Condition 9: (new) DERIVABLE, scoped out \u2014 four additional ad-hoc task-worker dispatch sites found by impact-analyst, filed as follow-up scope rather than blocking this item\\nDecision: APPROVED\\n</div>\\n\\n<div><sub>2026-08-28T14:00:25.779821Z</sub>\\n\\nRT-ICA Final: fix: Grooming-swarm subagent dispatch contract is inconsistent: one agent requires orchestrator-pasted content while the rest self-fetch, and nothing constrains what an orchestrator may add to a dispatch prompt\\nDate: 2026-08-28\\nGoal: Establish a documented rule for what belongs in a dynamic subagent dispatch prompt (item_ref/selector plus genuinely dynamic cross-agent findings) versus what belongs in the agent's own file or a shared skill, versus what the orchestrator must never add (speculative conclusions, pre-formed verdicts) \u2014 and bring the three found instances into line with it.\\nConditions:\\n1. backlog-item-groomer.md's ## Input section names literal content fields and contains zero backlog_view calls, despite mcp__plugin_dh_backlog tool access. | Snapshot: AVAILABLE -> Final: AVAILABLE | Citation: Fact-Check Claim 3/4 \u2014 backlog-item-groomer.md:48-53, tools list:4, zero backlog_view occurrences confirmed by grep.\\n2. impact-analyst.md, fact-checker.md, classifier.md, alignment-analyst.md each declare ## Input as only item_ref/selector and self-fetch via backlog_view. | Snapshot: AVAILABLE -> Final: AVAILABLE | Citation: Fact-Check Claim 1/2.\\n3. swarm.md's \\\"Groomer prompt\\\" section lists the full paste-in field set; sequence diagram uses looser phrasing for the other four agents. | Snapshot: AVAILABLE -> Final: AVAILABLE | Citation: Fact-Check Claim 5/6.\\n4. Classifier-anchoring incident \u2014 happened this session, nothing in classifier.md or swarm.md prohibits it. | Snapshot: AVAILABLE -> Final: AVAILABLE | Citation: session-observed at creation time.\\n5. close/start.md Step 5.6 (~line 121) re-types item title, plan address, and each acceptance criterion into a dh:task-worker prompt instead of self-fetch. | Snapshot: AVAILABLE -> Final: AVAILABLE | Citation: Fact-Check Claim 7/8.\\n6. Content of dh:subagent-contract \u2014 not yet read at snapshot time. | Snapshot: DERIVABLE -> Final: AVAILABLE | Citation: Read subagent-contract/SKILL.md directly \u2014 confirms zero constraint on dispatch-prompt content; correct, currently-empty home for the new rule.\\n7. Whether the same pattern exists in add-new-feature/implement-feature's specialist dispatches \u2014 unaudited. | Snapshot: DERIVABLE -> Final: DERIVABLE (scoped out) | Citation: Impact Radius confirms unaudited; orchestrator scoping decision \u2014 out of scope for this fix, recommend spot-check before merging the subagent-contract change.\\n8. task-worker.md's Identity vs. its ad-hoc dispatch use \u2014 mismatch. | Snapshot: DERIVABLE -> Final: DERIVABLE (scoped out) | Citation: Impact Radius confirms mismatch; orchestrator scoping decision \u2014 filed as follow-up, not folded into this fix.\\n9. (new) Four additional ad-hoc dh:task-worker dispatch sites: groom/error.md:18, groom-drift.md:36+65, quick/start.md:110+179, work/post-planning.md:14. | Snapshot: (absent) -> Final: DERIVABLE (scoped out) | Citation: Impact Radius scan; orchestrator scoping decision \u2014 follow-up item, not required for this item's completion.\\nAVAILABLE count: 6\\nDERIVABLE count: 3\\nMISSING count: 0\\nChanges from snapshot:\\n- Condition 6: DERIVABLE -> AVAILABLE (resolved by orchestrator reading subagent-contract/SKILL.md directly)\\n- Condition 9: (new) DERIVABLE, scoped out as follow-up\\nDecision: APPROVED\\n</div>\\n\\n## Issue Classification\\n\\n<div><sub>2026-08-28T13:43:35.001057Z</sub>\\n\\n**Type**: defect\\n\\n**Rationale**: Three concrete instances demonstrate a system-level bug in subagent dispatch contract enforcement. Four of five grooming-swarm agents (`impact-analyst`, `fact-checker`, `classifier`, `alignment-analyst`) establish a consistent pattern: declare only `item_ref`/`selector` as input and explicitly self-fetch the item via `backlog_view`. `backlog-item-groomer` breaks this pattern by requiring orchestrator-pasted content fields instead. The workflow orchestration doc (`swarm.md`) reinforces the over-pasting by explicitly listing content the orchestrator must paste \u2014 all of which are already sections on the item reachable via the same `backlog_view` call the other four agents make. This inconsistency creates a risk: nothing in the contracts constrains what else an orchestrator may add to a dispatch prompt beyond required input, allowing injection of speculative conclusions (demonstrated by the classifier-anchoring incident) that could bias independent verifiers.\\n\\n**Analysis Method**: Code inspection of agent input contracts, workflow documentation, and prompt dispatch patterns across the development-harness ecosystem. Root-cause analysis identifies a missing rule: no documented constraint governs what belongs in dynamic subagent dispatch prompts versus agent files versus what orchestrators must never add.\\n\\n**Scenario Target**: Orchestrator-managed subagent dispatch patterns, particularly independent verifiers (fact-checker, classifier, alignment-analyst) receiving pre-formed conclusions before reasoning. Also affects generic executors like `task-worker` when dispatched ad-hoc instead of via SAM task plans.\\n</div>\\n\\n## Files\\n\\n<div><sub>2026-08-28T14:00:25.780864Z</sub>\\n\\n- plugins/development-harness/agents/backlog-item-groomer.md \u2014 rewrite ## Input to item_ref/selector only; add explicit backlog_view self-fetch step.\\n- plugins/development-harness/skills/work-backlog-item/references/workflows/groom/swarm.md \u2014 shrink \\\"Groomer prompt\\\" section (~line 269) to item_ref; tighten sequence-diagram wording (~lines 65, 67, 68) for consistency across all five teammates.\\n- plugins/development-harness/skills/subagent-contract/SKILL.md \u2014 add the new dispatch-prompt-scope rule (self-fetch requirement + no-speculative-conclusions prohibition).\\n- plugins/development-harness/skills/work-backlog-item/references/workflows/close/start.md \u2014 rewrite Step 5.6 to dispatch dh:task-worker via reference + section name instead of manually re-typed content.\\n</div>\\n\\n## Resources\\n\\n<div><sub>2026-08-28T14:00:25.780884Z</sub>\\n\\n| Type | Item |\\n|------|------|\\n| Agent | @impact-analyst.md (reference self-fetch pattern) |\\n| Agent | @fact-checker.md (reference self-fetch pattern) |\\n| Agent | @classifier.md (reference self-fetch pattern; site of the anchoring incident) |\\n| Agent | @alignment-analyst.md (reference self-fetch pattern) |\\n| Agent | @task-worker.md (Identity section documents SAM-task-plan executor role, contrasts with its ad-hoc use) |\\n| Skill | dh:subagent-contract (fix target \u2014 new rule's home) |\\n| Prior work | plugins/development-harness/skills/work-backlog-item/references/workflows/groom/swarm.md |\\n| Prior work | plugins/development-harness/skills/work-backlog-item/references/workflows/close/start.md |\\n</div>\\n\\n## Impact\\n\\n<div><sub>2026-08-28T14:00:25.780187Z</sub>\\n\\n- Blocks: No functional workflow is blocked today \u2014 the paste-in path still works. What's at risk is the reliability of independent-verifier outputs (fact-checker, classifier, alignment-analyst) if an orchestrator ever exploits the undocumented gap to inject a pre-formed conclusion, and consistency across the 25 agent files that load dh:subagent-contract.\\n- Bottleneck: The groomer's exceptional (paste-in) contract adds orchestrator burden (the orchestrator must know and copy 10+ fields correctly) and is the one place in the swarm most likely to drift further from the other four agents over time.\\n</div>\\n\\n## Impact Radius\\n\\n<div><sub>2026-08-28T13:43:10.695421Z</sub>\\n\\n**Core inconsistency (must change together):**\\n- `plugins/development-harness/agents/backlog-item-groomer.md` \u2014 `## Input` section names literal content fields instead of `item_ref`; has zero `backlog_view` calls despite `mcp__plugin_dh_backlog` tool access. Primary fix target: add Phase 1 self-fetch, rewrite `## Input` to match the other four agents.\\n- `plugins/development-harness/skills/work-backlog-item/references/workflows/groom/swarm.md` \u2014 \\\"Groomer prompt\\\" section (~line 255) lists the full paste-in field set (title, description, source, priority, plan address, RT-ICA, Fact-Check, Issue Classification, Root-Cause Analysis, Impact Radius, Research) that must shrink to `item_ref` once the groomer self-fetches. Sequence diagram lines 65/67/68 (\\\"spawn (item details, ...)\\\", \\\"spawn (item description)\\\") need tightening to match the self-fetch contract already followed by the other four agents.\\n\\n**Reference pattern (should not regress, verify unaffected):**\\n- `plugins/development-harness/agents/impact-analyst.md`, `fact-checker.md`, `classifier.md`, `alignment-analyst.md` \u2014 each already declares `## Input` as `item_ref`/`selector` only and self-fetches via `backlog_view` in Phase 1. These are the target shape; no functional change expected, but any new shared-skill rule (see below) must not conflict with their existing Phase 1 wording.\\n\\n**Anchoring-risk instance (same root cause, no file with a text fix \u2014 needs a documented constraint):**\\n- No single file currently prohibits an orchestrator from writing pre-formed conclusions into a dispatch prompt (the classifier-anchoring incident from this session). The natural home is `plugins/development-harness/skills/subagent-contract/` (SKILL.md \u2014 confirm exact filename before editing), since it is the shared skill already loaded by all five groom-swarm agents. Because 25 agent files under `plugins/development-harness/agents/` load `subagent-contract` (grep-confirmed: generic-stage-agent, reviewer-security, code-reviewer, t0-baseline-capture, rtica-assessor, backlog-item-groomer, doc-drift-auditor, contract-verification, codebase-analyzer, reviewer-accessibility, reviewer-performance, service-docs-maintainer, alignment-analyst, feature-researcher, review-synthesizer, impact-analyst, classifier, integration-checker, plan-validator, reviewer-quality, swarm-task-planner, ecosystem-researcher, feature-verifier, fact-checker, task-worker, context-refinement, tn-verification-gate, dh-context-gathering), any new rule added there has a wide blast radius: it constrains every orchestrator that dispatches these 25 agents, not just the groom swarm. This argues for additive, narrowly-scoped wording (a \\\"what the orchestrator must never add\\\" rule) rather than a rewrite of the shared skill.\\n\\n**Second instance, different workflow (close):**\\n- `plugins/development-harness/skills/work-backlog-item/references/workflows/close/start.md` (Step 5.6, ~line 121) \u2014 re-types item title, plan address, and each acceptance criterion individually into a `dh:task-worker` prompt instead of passing `item_ref` + section name (`\\\"Acceptance Criteria\\\"`) for self-fetch via `backlog_view`.\\n\\n**Other ad-hoc `dh:task-worker` dispatch sites found during this scan (not named in the item's original report \u2014 new to Impact Radius, scope decision needed):**\\n- `plugins/development-harness/skills/work-backlog-item/references/workflows/groom/error.md:18` \u2014 `Agent(subagent_type=\\\"dh:task-worker\\\", prompt=\\\"...\\\")` with inline content, same pattern as close/start.md.\\n- `plugins/development-harness/skills/work-backlog-item/references/workflows/groom/groom-drift.md:36,65` \u2014 two more `dh:task-worker` (haiku) spawns with prompt-embedded content.\\n- `plugins/development-harness/skills/work-backlog-item/references/workflows/quick/start.md:110,179` \u2014 dispatches a `task-worker` agent by name; not yet checked for pasted-content vs. self-fetch, flagged for audit.\\n- `plugins/development-harness/skills/work-backlog-item/references/workflows/work/post-planning.md:14` \u2014 documents dispatching `dh:task-worker` per task; not yet checked.\\n\\n**Explicitly out of scope per RT-ICA condition 7 (confirmed, not further audited here):** `plugins/development-harness/skills/add-new-feature/SKILL.md` and `plugins/development-harness/skills/implement-feature/SKILL.md` also construct dynamic dispatch prompts (plan-validator, contract-verification, feature-verifier, codebase-analyzer) but were not audited for this item \u2014 a broader fix (e.g. a shared-skill rule) will affect them even without direct edits, so any change to `subagent-contract` should be spot-checked against at least one of these two files before merging.\\n\\n**Identity-mismatch tangent (RT-ICA condition 8, scoping call not yet made):** `plugins/development-harness/agents/task-worker.md` describes a SAM-task-plan executor (reads via `sam_task`, not ad-hoc prompts), but close/start.md, groom/error.md, and groom-drift.md all dispatch it in an ad-hoc prompt mode that doesn't match its own Identity section. Recommend treating this as a separate follow-up item rather than folding it into this fix \u2014 flagging here so grooming doesn't silently drop it.\\n\\n**No production code, tests, or CI paths affected** \u2014 every affected file is agent/skill markdown documentation (prompt contracts), not executable code.\\n</div>\\n\\n## Dependencies\\n\\n<div><sub>2026-08-28T14:00:25.780904Z</sub>\\n\\n- Depends on: None\\n- Blocks: None directly. Recommend filing two follow-up items after this one lands: (1) audit and fix the four additional ad-hoc dh:task-worker dispatch sites (groom/error.md:18, groom-drift.md:36,65, quick/start.md:110,179, work/post-planning.md:14) plus a spot-check of add-new-feature/implement-feature's specialist dispatches against the new rule; (2) resolve the task-worker.md Identity mismatch (SAM-task-plan executor vs. its ad-hoc prompt-dispatch use in close/groom workflows).\\n</div>\\n\\n## Priority\\n\\n<div><sub>2026-08-28T14:00:25.780159Z</sub>\\n\\n2/10 (P2, already set) - Internal contract-consistency defect in agent/workflow documentation; does not break the current groom workflow (paste-in still functions) and touches no production code, tests, or CI. Priority reflects architectural risk (anchoring bias reaching independent verifiers undetectably) rather than user-facing breakage.\\n\\nEffort: Small\\n</div>\\n\\n## Benefits\\n\\n<div><sub>2026-08-28T14:00:25.780206Z</sub>\\n\\n- Removes the single largest orchestrator-authored prompt in the groom swarm, cutting the surface area for accidental content staleness or mis-paste.\\n- Closes the specific anchoring vector that already fired once this session (classifier dispatch stating a conclusion before reasoning).\\n- Gives all 25 agents that load dh:subagent-contract one documented answer to \\\"what goes in a dispatch prompt,\\\" rather than leaving each new agent file to reinvent the convention by copying whichever sibling it happens to read first.\\n</div>\\n\\n## Design Intent Alignment\\n\\n<div><sub>2026-08-28T13:48:30.970851Z</sub>\\n\\n**MISSION_ALIGNED**\\n\\nThis item directly protects core development-harness design principles:\\n\\n1. **Independent Verification Integrity** \u2014 The development-harness mission relies on specialist verifiers (fact-checker, classifier, alignment-analyst) whose entire value derives from unbiased reasoning. The documented risk (orchestrators injecting pre-formed conclusions before agents reason independently) undermines this foundational architecture. This fix strengthens the constraint that guards independent verification.\\n\\n2. **Stateless Agent Methodology (SAM) Discipline** \u2014 CONTEXT.md defines SAM as \\\"complete context in, one verified artifact out, no memory carried between calls.\\\" The current contract gap allows orchestrators to violate this by pasting content plus additional framing that subtly biases the output. The fix documents proper dispatch contracts matching SAM's stateless principle.\\n\\n3. **Structured Dispatch Roles** \u2014 CONTEXT.md names explicit Orchestrator, Manager, and Worker roles with specific hand-off discipline. The pattern discovered here (4 of 5 groom-swarm agents self-fetch via `backlog_view`, but groomer requires paste-in) represents a breach of this discipline. Fixing it restores consistency in how dispatch contracts work across all 25 agents that load `subagent-contract`.\\n\\n4. **Consistency as Systemic Signal** \u2014 The README states \\\"every edit improves product design; errors and linting issues are architectural signals.\\\" The inconsistency (established pattern vs. exception) combined with the anchoring-risk incident (orchestrator bias) surfaces a systemic contract gap affecting multiple workflows. The fix addresses the root cause (missing documented rule) rather than patching each instance.\\n\\n5. **Quality Gate Layer Protection** \u2014 The development-harness verification stages (S6 Forensic Review, S7 Final Verification, multi-perspective review) depend on independent evaluation. When independent verifiers receive pre-formed conclusions in their dispatch prompts, the entire quality gate layer becomes subject to confirmation bias rather than genuine verification. The fix preserves the integrity of this layer.\\n\\n6. **Documented Constraints as Machine-Readable Contracts** \u2014 The harness architecture treats shared skills like `subagent-contract` as the control surface for dispatch rules. Adding a \\\"what the orchestrator must never add\\\" constraint to this shared skill (loaded by all 25 agents) is the proper architectural home for this rule, not a one-off patch in individual agent files.\\n\\n**Rationale**: This is defensive architecture \u2014 strengthening the documented contracts that prevent orchestrator-induced bias in independent verifiers. It aligns with the principle that \\\"structured process coordination\\\" (spelled out in dispatch role vocabulary and SAM methodology) is how development-harness prevents the predictable breakdowns it was designed to eliminate.\\n\\n</div>\\n\\n## Acceptance Criteria\\n\\n<div><sub>2026-08-28T14:00:25.780283Z</sub>\\n\\n1. backlog-item-groomer.md's ## Input section names only item_ref/selector (matching the other four groom-swarm agents) and the file contains an explicit step instructing a mcp__plugin_dh_backlog__backlog_view self-fetch call before discovery begins \u2014 verified by reading the file and confirming no literal content fields remain in ## Input and a backlog_view call exists early in ## Process.\\n2. swarm.md's \\\"Groomer prompt\\\" section no longer lists the paste-in field set (title, description, source, priority, plan address, RT-ICA, Fact-Check, Issue Classification, Root-Cause Analysis, Impact Radius, Research) and instead names item_ref as the required input; the sequence diagram lines for the groomer and the other four agents use consistent self-fetch phrasing \u2014 verified by reading the updated section and diagram.\\n3. dh:subagent-contract's SKILL.md contains a new rule stating: (a) a dispatch prompt carries only the item reference plus genuinely dynamic cross-agent findings not retrievable via backlog_view, (b) an agent with mcp__plugin_dh_backlog tool access must self-fetch rather than receive item content pasted in, and (c) an orchestrator must never write a speculative conclusion or pre-formed verdict into a dispatch prompt \u2014 verified by reading the file and confirming the rule text is present and specific enough to flag a concrete violating example (e.g. the classifier-anchoring incident's actual prompt text) as non-compliant.\\n4. close/start.md Step 5.6 dispatches dh:task-worker by passing the item reference and section=\\\"Acceptance Criteria\\\" for self-fetch via backlog_view, rather than the orchestrator extracting and re-typing title, plan address, and each criterion into the prompt \u2014 verified by reading the updated step.\\n5. Regression check: impact-analyst.md, fact-checker.md, classifier.md, alignment-analyst.md remain unedited (or, if dh:subagent-contract changes require touching them, their existing Phase 1 self-fetch wording is preserved) \u2014 verified by diff review confined to the four named files plus backlog-item-groomer.md, swarm.md, subagent-contract/SKILL.md, and close/start.md.\\n6. Negative/edge case: given the classifier-anchoring incident's actual prompt text (\\\"this is very likely defect ... confirm this classification yourself\\\") as a test case, the new subagent-contract rule text, read literally, identifies it as a violation \u2014 not merely \\\"should avoid bias\\\" in the abstract, but specific enough to catch this concrete example.\\n</div>\\n\\n## Expected Behavior\\n\\n<div><sub>2026-08-28T14:00:25.780228Z</sub>\\n\\nAn orchestrator dispatching any of the five groom-swarm agents (and any of the other 25 dh:subagent-contract-consuming agents) passes only the item reference and any information that genuinely cannot be self-fetched (e.g., cross-teammate findings produced this run and not yet on the item). Every agent with mcp__plugin_dh_backlog tool access self-fetches its own item content via backlog_view rather than receiving it pasted in. No dispatch prompt states a conclusion, verdict, or biased framing for an agent whose value is independent judgment \u2014 dh:subagent-contract documents this as an explicit, checkable rule rather than leaving it implicit in four-out-of-five agents' individual files.\\n</div>\\n\\n## Effort\\n\\n<div><sub>2026-08-28T14:00:25.780922Z</sub>\\n\\nSmall - Four markdown files (one agent, one shared skill, two workflow docs), no code/tests/CI. Mechanical rewrite of an ## Input section, a prompt-assembly paragraph, a sequence diagram, and one new rule paragraph in an already-short shared skill file.\\n</div>\\n\\n## Reproducibility\\n\\n<div><sub>2026-08-28T14:00:25.780083Z</sub>\\n\\n1. Open plugins/development-harness/agents/backlog-item-groomer.md and read ## Input \u2014 it lists literal content fields (item title, item description, research questions, RT-ICA summary) instead of item_ref/selector.\\n2. Grep the same file for backlog_view \u2014 zero matches, despite the tools: frontmatter (line 4) already listing mcp__plugin_dh_backlog.\\n3. Open impact-analyst.md, fact-checker.md, classifier.md, alignment-analyst.md and confirm each declares ## Input as only item_ref/selector with an explicit Phase 1 backlog_view self-fetch call (e.g. impact-analyst.md:22-26).\\n4. Open swarm.md, \\\"Groomer prompt\\\" section (~line 269) \u2014 it lists 10+ fields the orchestrator must paste for the groomer, versus terser dispatch phrasing for the other four agents in the sequence diagram (~lines 65-68).\\n5. Open close/start.md Step 5.6 (~line 121) \u2014 it manually extracts and re-types item title, plan address, and each acceptance criterion into a dh:task-worker prompt instead of passing item_ref + section=\\\"Acceptance Criteria\\\" for self-fetch.\\n6. Open subagent-contract/SKILL.md \u2014 confirm it has no rule addressing dispatch-prompt content scope or prohibiting orchestrator-injected conclusions.\\n</div>\\n\\n## Context\\n\\n<div><sub>0000-00-00T00:00:00Z</sub>\\n\\n- **Source**: Session observation \u2014 development-harness groom workflow for #3169\\n- **Priority**: P2\\n- **Added**: 2026-08-28\\n- **Research questions**: None\\n</div>\\n\\n## Root-Cause Analysis\\n\\n<div><sub>2026-08-28T13:45:32.205306Z</sub>\\n\\n**Root Cause**: No documented rule governs subagent dispatch prompt construction discipline. The pattern is proven (four agents establish self-fetch via `backlog_view`); the inconsistency is the symptom, not the disease.\\n\\n**Evidence Chain**:\\n\\n1. **Established pattern**: `impact-analyst.md`, `fact-checker.md`, `classifier.md`, `alignment-analyst.md` each declare `## Input` as only `item_ref`/`selector` and contain explicit Phase 1 `backlog_view` calls to self-fetch the item.\\n\\n2. **Pattern break**: `backlog-item-groomer.md` declares `## Input` with literal content fields (title, description, research questions, RT-ICA summary) despite having `mcp__plugin_dh_backlog` tool access. Contains zero `backlog_view` calls.\\n\\n3. **Workflow doc reinforcement**: `skills/work-backlog-item/references/workflows/groom/swarm.md` \\\"Groomer prompt\\\" section explicitly lists 10+ fields orchestrator must paste (title, description, source, priority, plan address, RT-ICA, Fact-Check, Issue Classification, Root-Cause Analysis, Impact Radius, Research section) \u2014 all reachable via `backlog_view(selector=item_ref, section=\\\"...\\\")` that the other four agents already call themselves.\\n\\n4. **Contract gap**: Neither `swarm.md` nor agent files constrain what else an orchestrator may add beyond required input fields. During this session's classification run, orchestrator injected speculative conclusion (\\\"very likely defect\\\") into classifier prompt before classifier reasoned independently. No contract violation detected.\\n\\n5. **Pattern recurrence**: `skills/work-backlog-item/references/workflows/close/start.md` Step 5.6 extracts and re-types item title, plan address, and acceptance criteria into `dh:task-worker` prompt instead of passing `item_ref` + section name for self-fetch, despite `task-worker` having full `mcp__plugin_dh_backlog` access.\\n\\n**Anchoring Risk**: Independent verifiers (fact-checker, classifier, alignment-analyst) whose entire value derives from unbiased reasoning can receive pre-formed conclusions in dispatch prompts. The risk is structural, not detectable post-hoc: written sections look independent either way.\\n\\n**Upgrade Path**: Document a rule in `dh:subagent-contract` (loaded by 4 of 5 groom-swarm agents) specifying: (1) required input fields only (item_ref/selector, genuinely dynamic cross-agent findings), (2) agents with MCP backlog access must self-fetch via `backlog_view`, (3) orchestrators must never add speculative conclusions or pre-formed verdicts. Apply rule to all three instances: backlog-item-groomer, close/start verification-agent dispatch, and affected specialist dispatches across other workflows.\\n</div>\\n\\n## Desired Structure\\n\\n<div><sub>2026-08-28T14:00:25.780257Z</sub>\\n\\n- backlog-item-groomer.md's ## Input and process match the shape already used by impact-analyst.md/fact-checker.md/classifier.md/alignment-analyst.md: item_ref/selector only, plus an explicit self-fetch step.\\n- swarm.md's Groomer prompt section and sequence diagram describe a thin dispatch (item_ref plus any non-retrievable dynamic findings), matching the other four teammates' dispatch descriptions.\\n- dh:subagent-contract carries a new, narrowly-scoped rule usable by all 25 consuming agents: dispatch prompts carry only the reference plus genuinely dynamic findings; agents with backlog MCP access self-fetch; orchestrators never inject speculative conclusions or pre-formed verdicts.\\n- close/start.md Step 5.6 dispatches dh:task-worker with the item reference and target section name for self-fetch, not manually re-typed content.\\n</div>\\n\\n## Output / Evidence\\n\\n<div><sub>2026-08-28T14:00:25.780126Z</sub>\\n\\n- backlog-item-groomer.md:48-53 (Input section), :4 (tools list) \u2014 confirmed by direct read.\\n- impact-analyst.md:22-26 \u2014 self-fetch pattern, confirmed by direct read.\\n- swarm.md:269-274 (\\\"Groomer prompt\\\") and :65,67,68 (sequence diagram) \u2014 confirmed by direct read.\\n- close/start.md:99-121 (Step 5.6) \u2014 confirmed by direct read.\\n- subagent-contract/SKILL.md (full file, 44 lines) \u2014 confirmed empty of dispatch-scope rules by direct read.\\n- task-worker.md:12-16 (Identity: SAM-task-plan executor) versus its ad-hoc use in close/start.md/groom/error.md/groom-drift.md \u2014 confirmed by direct read.\\n</div>\\n\\n## Human Input\\n\\n<div><sub>2026-08-28T14:00:25.780824Z</sub>\\n\\nNone required \u2014 RT-ICA is APPROVED and all four target files plus the fix's shape were confirmed by direct read.\\n</div>\\n\\n## Artifact Classification\\n\\n<div><sub>2026-08-28T14:00:25.779937Z</sub>\\n\\n- Primary artifact type: Documentation / Agent (a shared skill file and three agent/workflow markdown files \u2014 no executable code, no tests, no CI)\\n- Behavioral boundary: What an orchestrator writes into a dispatched subagent's prompt, and what a self-fetching agent's own Input/Phase 1 instructions require it to do\\n- Domain-semantic evaluation target: Whether the edited files, read literally, produce the correct dispatch behavior \u2014 a self-fetch instruction that actually appears and is followed, and a prohibition specific enough that a reviewer could point to a violating prompt and say \\\"that breaks the rule\\\"\\n</div>\\n\",\"digest\":\"cdba5caad27416ab58e8469d145e6eeab62519446b341a2bb67c97ea596c5aaa\",\"comment_id\":\"IC_kwDOQGimCs8AAAABRQzrrg\"}"}